\title{Multinomial Logistic Regression versus\\$k$-Nearest Neighbours on Iris}
\author{Anonymous Author(s)}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We compare multinomial logistic regression (LR) with $k$-nearest neighbours
($k$NN, $k$ chosen by inner cross-validation) on the Iris data (OpenML id
\DataID) by 10 repetitions of stratified 5-fold cross-validation. With
corrected resampled-$t$ 95\% intervals, LR reaches \LRMean\% [\LRLo, \LRHi] and
$k$NN \KNNMean\% [\KNNLo, \KNNHi]. The paired difference LR$-$$k$NN is
\DiffMean\pp{} [\DiffLo, \DiffHi]: the interval covers zero, so the data do not
distinguish the two methods (which is not evidence that they are equivalent). The full cross-validation was run as a CPU
job on the Ibex cluster (node \texttt{\IbexNode}) and locally; the two
runs gave \IdenticalVerdict{} numbers.
\end{abstract}

\section{Setup}
We ask how far apart a linear and an instance-based classifier
\citep{cover1967} are on Iris \citep{fisher1936} once $k$ is tuned without
leakage and fold dependence is accounted for.

\textbf{Data.} OpenML \citep{vanschoren2014openml} dataset \DataID{}
(\texttt{\DataName}, version \DataVersion), ARFF downloaded from OpenML by our code (md5 equal to OpenML's, sha256 \texttt{\DataSHAShort\dots});
150 flowers, 4 numeric features, 3 classes of 50. Used as published:
three duplicate rows kept, two entries differing from Fisher's values
\citep{bezdek1999} not corrected.

\textbf{Methods} (scikit-learn \VerSklearn{} \citep{pedregosa2011sklearn}).
Both pipelines standardise features, fitted on the training fold only.
LR: L2 penalty, $C=1$, lbfgs, softmax (multinomial) loss, checked on the
fitted model; not tuned. $k$NN: Euclidean distance, uniform weights; $k$ is chosen
from \KGrid{} by stratified 5-fold inner cross-validation on each outer training
set only \citep{varma2006}, ties to the smallest $k$.

\textbf{Protocol.} Stratified 5-fold cross-validation repeated \NRepeats{}
times (seed \OuterSeed), $J=\NFolds$ outer folds of \NTrain{} training and
\NTest{} test cases; both methods are scored on identical splits, so the
differences $d_f$ are paired by fold. Intervals use the corrected resampled $t$
\citep{nadeau2003,bouckaert2004}: $\bar x \pm t_{0.975,\CorrDF}\,
\sqrt{(1/J + n_\text{test}/n_\text{train})\,s^2}$ with
$n_\text{test}/n_\text{train} = \nicefrac{1}{4}$, applied to each method's fold
accuracies and to $d_f$. All runs are single-threaded.

