LR's mean accuracy is \DiffMean\pp{} higher (Table~\ref{tab:main},
Figure~\ref{fig:main}), but the fold-level differences spread widely (SD
\DiffFoldSD\pp): LR wins \FoldsLRBetter{} folds, $k$NN \FoldsKNNBetter, and
\FoldsTie{} are ties, and the corrected interval [\DiffLo, \DiffHi] covers zero
($p=\DiffP$). A naive $t$ interval over the 50 folds, [\DiffNaiveLo,
\DiffNaiveHi], would exclude zero; it treats overlapping folds as independent
and is shown only as a warning. Inner cross-validation chose $k$ between
\KMin{} and \KMax{} (median \KMedian; $k=\KMode$ in \KModeCount{} of \NFolds{}
folds). Checks outside the pre-specified plan (no standardisation:
\SensDiffRawMean\pp{} [\SensDiffRawLo, \SensDiffRawHi]; LR's $C$ tuned:
\SensDiffTunedMean\pp{} [\SensDiffTunedLo, \SensDiffTunedHi]) change the size
of the lead, not its sign or the interval covering zero.

\textbf{Reproducibility.} The full cross-validation ran as a single-CPU Slurm
job on Ibex (job \IbexJobID, node \texttt{\IbexNode}) and locally, with Python \IbexPython{} and \LocalPython{} respectively, the same
locked packages (numpy \VerNumpy, scipy \VerScipy, scikit-learn \VerSklearn)
and one thread. The two runs gave \IdenticalVerdict{} numbers: all predicted
labels, selected $k$, fold accuracies and printed values agree exactly. An
earlier replicate (job \OtherIbexJobID, node \texttt{\OtherIbexNode}) also
reproduced every reported number; only its LR probabilities differed (by up to
\OtherMaxAbsDiffProb), from a different OpenBLAS kernel (\OtherIbexBLAS{} vs.\
\LocalBLAS). The code that produced every number, the table and the
figure is in \texttt{code/} and \texttt{figures/} next to this PDF;
\texttt{README.md} maps each value to its script and result file.

\textbf{Limitations.} Corrected resampled-$t$ intervals are approximate and
may still under-cover for cross-validation estimates \citep{bates2024}; Iris
is small and nearly separable, so the comparison says little about other data;
LR is not tuned.

